---
import "../styles/global.css";
import data from "../data/viz.json";

const { meta } = data;
const int = (x: number | null) => (x == null ? "–" : x.toLocaleString("en-US"));
const pct = (x: number | null, d = 2) => (x == null ? "–" : `${(x * 100).toFixed(d)} %`);
const r = (x: number | null) => (x == null ? "–" : x.toFixed(2));
const rStyle = (x: number | null) =>
  x == null ? "" : `background: color-mix(in oklab, var(${x >= 0 ? "--div-cool" : "--div-warm"}) ${Math.round(Math.abs(x) * 55)}%, transparent)`;

const parties = ["CDU/CSU", "SPD", "GRÜNE", "FDP", "DIE LINKE", "AfD", "BSW"];
const topMonths = [...data.monthly]
  .filter((d) => d.sentences != null && d.sentences >= 2000)
  .sort((a, b) => b.share - a.share)
  .slice(0, 15);
const corrShare = data.correlations.filter((d) => d.target === "Climate sentence share");
const corrSpeech = data.correlations.filter((d) => d.target === "Climate speech share");
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Bundestag Climate Explorer</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <header class="top">
        <div>
          <h1>Heat Without Debate?</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Climate change in the German Bundestag next to the physical climate signal and public attention,{" "}
            {meta.first_month} to {meta.last_month}. Exploratory view of the pipeline outputs so far.
          </p>
        </div>
        <button class="theme" id="theme-toggle" type="button">Toggle theme</button>
      </header>

      <p class="note">
        <strong>Provisional:</strong> “climate-related” means a sentence contains one of the {meta.keywords.core.length}{" "}
        <em>core</em> keywords from <code>config/keywords_de.yaml</code> (simple substring match). This is a stand-in until
        step 2 (<code>src/02_relevance.py</code>) exists. Stance (step 3) is not available yet.
      </p>

      <div class="tiles">
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{int(meta.speeches)}</div><div class="label">speeches</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{(meta.sentences / 1e6).toFixed(2)} M</div><div class="label">speech sentences</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{int(meta.climate_sentences)}</div><div class="label">climate sentences ({pct(meta.climate_sentences / meta.sentences)})</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{meta.n_months}</div><div class="label">months ({meta.session_months} with plenary sessions)</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{meta.trend_terms.length}</div><div class="label">Google Trends series</div></div>
      </div>

      <nav class="toc">
        <a href="#signals">Three signals</a><a href="#indexed">Indexed</a><a href="#correlation">Correlations</a>
        <a href="#trends">Google Trends</a><a href="#temperature">Temperature</a><a href="#parties">Parties</a>
        <a href="#government">Government vs opposition</a><a href="#keywords">Keywords</a><a href="#sceptic">Sceptic markers</a>
        <a href="#volume">Debate volume</a><a href="#top">Top months</a><a href="#notes">Notes</a>
      </nav>

      <section id="signals">
        <h2>Three signals on one timeline</h2>
        <p class="sub">Monthly values as faint bars or lines, 12-month rolling average as the solid line. Hover for values.</p>
        <div class="card"><h3>Bundestag: share of speech sentences that are climate-related</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-share"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Public attention: Google Trends “Klimawandel” (search term, DE)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-trends-main"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Physical signal: temperature anomaly Germany (DWD)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-temp-main"></div></div>
      </section>

      <section id="indexed">
        <h2>Smoothed and indexed</h2>
        <p class="sub">
          12-month rolling averages, each converted to z-scores (standard deviations from its own mean) so the four
          series share one axis. This compares shape, not size.
        </p>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="legend">
            <span style="--c: var(--series-1)">Bundestag climate share</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-2)">Trends: Klimawandel</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-3)">Temperature DE</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-4)">Temperature global</span>
          </div>
          <div class="chart" id="chart-indexed"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="correlation">
        <h2>Correlations</h2>
        <p class="sub">
          Each dot is one month with plenary sessions; the line is a least-squares fit. Pearson r below, with the
          Bundestag series lagged 0, 1, 3 and 12 months behind the driver. Descriptive only: all series trend upward
          and are autocorrelated, so these numbers overstate any causal link.
        </p>
        <div class="grid-3">
          <div class="card"><h3>vs. Google Trends “Klimawandel”</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-scatter-trends"></div></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>vs. temperature anomaly DE</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-scatter-de"></div></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>vs. temperature anomaly global</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-scatter-global"></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          {[["Climate sentence share (monthly)", corrShare, true], ["Climate speech share (monthly)", corrSpeech, false]].map(([title, rows, smoothed]) => (
            <div class="card table-wrap">
              <h3>{title}</h3>
              <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Driver</th><th>lag 0</th><th>lag 1</th><th>lag 3</th><th>lag 12</th>{smoothed && <th>12m smoothed</th>}</tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {(rows as typeof corrShare).map((d) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{d.driver}</td>
                      <td class="r" style={rStyle(d.lag0)}>{r(d.lag0)}</td>
                      <td class="r" style={rStyle(d.lag1)}>{r(d.lag1)}</td>
                      <td class="r" style={rStyle(d.lag3)}>{r(d.lag3)}</td>
                      <td class="r" style={rStyle(d.lag12)}>{r(d.lag12)}</td>
                      {smoothed && <td class="r" style={rStyle(d.lag0_12m)}>{r(d.lag0_12m)}</td>}
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          “Climate speech share” = share of speeches with at least one climate sentence. Smoothed correlations use
          overlapping 12-month windows and look much stronger than they are.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="trends">
        <h2>Google Trends, all four exports</h2>
        <p class="sub">Each file was exported alone, so each has its own 0–100 scale. Compare shapes, not levels across panels.</p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          {meta.trend_terms.map((t) => (
            <div class="card"><h3>{t}</h3><div class="chart" id={`chart-trend-${t}`}></div></div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          <strong>Check the exports:</strong> “Klimawandel” jumps to 100 in 2022-04 and “Klimaschutz” in 2023-04 while the
          months around sit near 10. Single-month spikes like this are often sampling glitches; a re-export on another
          day would show whether they are real. They also lift the 12-month averages for a full year.
          “Klima” (topic) also covers weather and air conditioning, which may explain why it moves differently.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="temperature">
        <h2>Temperature anomalies</h2>
        <p class="sub">Monthly deviation from the 1991–2020 mean of the same calendar month. Warm months red, cool months blue, 12-month average as line.</p>
        <div class="card"><h3>Germany (DWD area mean)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-temp-de"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Global (NASA GISTEMP v4, re-baselined)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-temp-global"></div></div>
      </section>

      <section id="parties">
        <h2>Parties</h2>
        <p class="sub">Share of each party's speech sentences that are climate-related, per year (other parties in gray for reference). Years with fewer than 2,000 sentences of a party are left out.</p>
        <div class="grid-4">
          {parties.map((p) => (
            <div class="card"><h3>{p}</h3><div class="chart" id={`chart-party-${p}`}></div></div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Whole period, all years combined</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-party-total"></div></div>
      </section>

      <section id="government">
        <h2>Government vs opposition</h2>
        <p class="sub">Climate share of speech sentences by whether the speaker's party was in the federal coalition on that day (<code>config/governments.yaml</code>).</p>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="legend">
            <span style="--c: var(--series-1)">Government parties</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-2)">Opposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="chart" id="chart-gov"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="keywords">
        <h2>Keywords over time</h2>
        <p class="sub">Number of speech sentences containing each dictionary term, per year. Groups top to bottom: core, energy, sceptic markers.</p>
        <div class="card"><div class="chart" id="chart-keywords"></div></div>
      </section>

      <section id="sceptic">
        <h2>Sceptic markers</h2>
        <p class="sub">
          Sentences with “Klimahysterie”, “Klimareligion”, “Klimapanik” or “Klimaideologie”. Only {int(meta.sceptic_sentences)} in total,
          so this is a hint for stance annotation, not a measure.
        </p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="card"><h3>Per year</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-sceptic-year"></div></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Per party, whole period</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-sceptic-party"></div></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="volume">
        <h2>Debate volume</h2>
        <p class="sub">All speech sentences per month (the denominator of the climate share). Gaps are recess months; dashed lines mark new legislative periods.</p>
        <div class="card"><h3>Speech sentences per month</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-volume"></div></div>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Legislative period</th><th>months</th><th>speeches</th><th>sentences</th><th>climate sentences</th><th>climate share</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.lp.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.lp}. Bundestag</td><td>{d.first} – {d.last}</td><td>{int(d.speeches)}</td><td>{int(d.sentences)}</td><td>{int(d.climate)}</td><td>{pct(d.share)}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>speeches</th><th>sentences</th><th>climate sentences</th><th>climate share</th><th>sceptic markers</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.party_total.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.party}</td><td>{int(d.speeches)}</td><td>{int(d.sentences)}</td><td>{int(d.climate)}</td><td>{pct(d.share)}</td><td>{int(d.sceptic)}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="top">
        <h2>Months with the highest climate share</h2>
        <p class="sub">Months with at least 2,000 speech sentences, so single short sittings don't dominate.</p>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>climate share</th><th>climate sentences</th><th>all sentences</th><th>Trends: Klimawandel</th><th>anomaly DE</th><th>anomaly global</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {topMonths.map((d) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{d.month}</td><td>{pct(d.share)}</td><td>{int(d.climate)}</td><td>{int(d.sentences)}</td>
                  <td>{d.Klimawandel ?? "–"}</td><td>{d.anomaly_de?.toFixed(2) ?? "–"} °C</td><td>{d.anomaly_global?.toFixed(2) ?? "–"} °C</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="notes">
        <h2>Notes and caveats</h2>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li>Speeches: PoliCorp-GermaParl2-PLUS (15th–20th Bundestag) plus the 21st Bundestag converted from Bundestag Open Data XML. Interjections excluded; speaker roles MPs, government, Bundesrat.</li>
          <li>Climate relevance is a provisional keyword match on the <em>core</em> list only. Substring matching means e.g. “Klimaschutzgesetz” counts, but so would false hits. Replace with step 2 output.</li>
          <li>Many protocols write CO₂ as “CO 2” (all of 2009, most of 2008, 2018 and 2019), which the keyword “co2” misses. This page treats “CO 2” as “co2”; step 2 needs the same fix.</li>
          <li>The sentence share depends on how sentences are split; PoliCorp and the XML converter split slightly differently (not yet validated on this machine).</li>
          <li>Correlations are descriptive Pearson coefficients on monthly data, not models. Time-series methods (detrending, lags, ARIMA/VAR) belong in the real analysis.</li>
          <li>Google Trends values are sampled by Google; a re-export on another day can differ slightly.</li>
          <li>Data ends {meta.last_month} (<code>end_date</code> in <code>config/settings.yaml</code>).</li>
          <li>Rebuild the data: <code>python viz/export_data.py</code>, then <code>npm run dev</code> in <code>viz/</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>

    <script>
      import data from "../data/viz.json";
      import { render } from "../scripts/charts.js";

      const root = document.documentElement;
      try {
        const saved = localStorage.getItem("theme");
        if (saved) root.dataset.theme = saved;
      } catch {}
      document.getElementById("theme-toggle")!.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const dark = root.dataset.theme ? root.dataset.theme === "dark" : matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;
        root.dataset.theme = dark ? "light" : "dark";
        try { localStorage.setItem("theme", root.dataset.theme); } catch {}
        render(data);
      });
      matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").addEventListener("change", () => render(data));

      render(data);

      let lastWidth = 0;
      let timer: number | undefined;
      new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
        const w = Math.round(entry.contentRect.width);
        if (w === lastWidth) return;
        lastWidth = w;
        clearTimeout(timer);
        timer = window.setTimeout(() => render(data), 120);
      }).observe(document.querySelector("main")!);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
